            \n                                <\/mml:mo>\n                                <mml:mi>q<\/mml:mi>\n                                <mml:mrow class=\"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD\">\n                                  <mml:mo>\/<\/mml:mo>\n                                <\/mml:mrow>\n                                <mml:mn>2<\/mml:mn>\n                                <mml:mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">\n                                  \u230b\n                                  \n                                <\/mml:mo>\n                              <\/mml:mrow>\n                            <\/mml:munderover>\n                            <mml:mfrac>\n                              <mml:mrow>\n                                <mml:mi>\n                                  \u03c7\n                                  \n                                <\/mml:mi>\n                                <mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mml:mo>\n                                <mml:mi>n<\/mml:mi>\n                                <mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mml:mo>\n                              <\/mml:mrow>\n                              <mml:msup>\n                                <mml:mi>n<\/mml:mi>\n                                <mml:mi>s<\/mml:mi>\n                              <\/mml:msup>\n                            <\/mml:mfrac>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">T_\\nu (s,\\chi ):=\\sum _{n=\\nu q+\\lfloor q\/2 \\rfloor +1}^{(\\nu +1) q+\\lfloor q\/2 \\rfloor } \\frac {\\chi (n)}{n^s}<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    considered previously by Leu and Li for\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math\/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" alttext=\"s equals 1\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>s<\/mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                            <mml:mn>1<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">s=1<\/mml:annotation>\n                        <\/mml:semantics>\n                      <\/mml:math>\n                    <\/inline-formula>\n                    . These explicit estimates are all rather tight and may have independent interests.\n                  <\/p>","DOI":"10.1090\/s0025-5718-04-01701-6","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":5,"title":["Real zeros of Dedekind zeta functions of real quadratic fields"],"author":[{"given":"Kok","family":"Chua","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Mathematics of Computation"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2004,7,21]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1090\/s0025-5718-04-01701-6"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s00209-016-1643-8","title":["On complex zeros off the critical line for non-monomial polynomial of zeta-functions"],"author":[{"given":"Takashi","family":"Nakamura","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"\u0141ukasz","family":"Pa\u0144kowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016,4,7]]},"container-title":["Mathematische Zeitschrift"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":2,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s00209-016-1643-8"},{"DOI":"10.1049\/pi-c.1960.0015","title":["Some tests for the number of positive zeros and for the numbers of real and complex zeros of a real polynomial"],"author":[{"given":"O.P.D.","family":"Cutteridge","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1960]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the IEE Part C: Monographs"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":2,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1049\/pi-c.1960.0015"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>When we look at the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function and their deep connection to prime numbers, we see a universe stretching infinitely outward. As we climb higher up the critical line, the gaps between these zeros shrink. But rather than collapsing into nothingness, something remarkable happens: their interaction locks into a precise, constant equilibrium. This paper introduces a new conceptual framework the Zeta Circle Equation which demonstrates how an infinite squeeze always loops back to a perfect circular constant.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7574519","type":"posted-content","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Zeta-Circle Theorem: The Circular Equilibrium of Riemann Zeta Zeros and Prime Frequencies"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2647-440X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"H.D.T.D.","family":"Amarathunga","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7574519"},{"DOI":"10.4064\/aa-14-2-117-140","title":["Real zeros of the Dedekind zeta function of an imaginary quadratic field"],"author":[{"given":"M.E.","family":"Low","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1968]]},"container-title":["Acta Arithmetica"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":12,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4064\/aa-14-2-117-140"},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>Let 0 &lt; <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic> \u2a7d 1, <jats:italic>s, z<\/jats:italic> \u2208 <jats:inline-formula><jats:alternatives><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" mime-subtype=\"gif\" xlink:type=\"simple\" xlink:href=\"S0305004115000547_inline1\"\/><jats:tex-math>${\\mathbb{C}}$<\/jats:tex-math><\/jats:alternatives><\/jats:inline-formula> and 0 &lt; |<jats:italic>z<\/jats:italic>| \u2a7d 1. Then the Hurwitz\u2013Lerch zeta function is defined by \u03a6(<jats:italic>s<\/jats:italic>, <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic>, <jats:italic>z<\/jats:italic>) \u2254 \u2211<jats:sup>\u221e<\/jats:sup><jats:sub><jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic> = 0<\/jats:sub><jats:italic>z<jats:sup>n<\/jats:sup><\/jats:italic>(<jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic> + <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic>)<jats:sup>\u2212 <jats:italic>s<\/jats:italic><\/jats:sup> when \u03c3 \u2254 \u211c(<jats:italic>s<\/jats:italic>) &gt; 1. In this paper, we show that the Hurwitz zeta function \u03b6(\u03c3, <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic>) \u2254 \u03a6(\u03c3, <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic>, 1) does not vanish for all 0 &lt; \u03c3 &lt; 1 if and only if <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic> \u2a7e 1\/2. Moreover, we prove that \u03a6(\u03c3, <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic>, <jats:italic>z<\/jats:italic>) \u2260 0 for all 0 &lt; \u03c3 &lt; 1 and 0 &lt; <jats:italic>a<\/jats:italic> \u2a7d 1 when <jats:italic>z<\/jats:italic> \u2260 1. Real zeros of Hurwitz\u2013Lerch type of Euler\u2013Zagier double zeta functions are studied as well.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1017\/s0305004115000547","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":8,"title":["Real zeros of Hurwitz\u2013Lerch zeta and Hurwitz\u2013Lerch type of Euler\u2013Zagier double zeta functions"],"author":[{"given":"TAKASHI","family":"NAKAMURA","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"container-title":["Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015,10,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/s0305004115000547"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/1145768.1145819","title":["Locating real multiple zeros of a real interval polynomial"],"author":[{"given":"Hiroshi","family":"Sekigawa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corporation, Kanagawa, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kiyoshi","family":"Shirayanagi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corporation, Kanagawa, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,7,9]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2006 international symposium on Symbolic and algebraic computation"],"type":"proceedings-article","is-referenced-by-count":8,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/1145768.1145819"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>An algorithm is described based on Newton's method which simultaneously approximates all zeros of a polynomial with only real zeros. The algorithm, which is conceptually suitable for parallel computation, determines its own starting values so that convergence to the zeros is guaranteed. Multiple zeros and their multiplicity are readily determined. At no point in the method is polynomial deflation used.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/355606.361872","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":7,"title":["A highly parallel algorithm for approximating all zeros of a polynomial with only real zeros"],"author":[{"given":"Merrell L.","family":"Patrick","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Duke Univ., Durham, NC"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Communications of the ACM"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1972,11]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/355606.361872"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/00029890.2008.11920507","title":["Bounds on the Zeros of the Derivative of a Polynomial with All Real Zeros"],"author":[{"given":"Aaron","family":"Melman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Applied Mathematics, Santa Clara University, Santa Clara, CA 95053"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,2]]},"container-title":["The American Mathematical Monthly"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":3,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/00029890.2008.11920507"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Recent experiments realise the nontrivial zeros of \u03b6 as dynamical quantum phase transitions, with Re(s) an inverse temperature and Im(s) an evolution time. That is the pairing which organises Thorin\u2019s representation of a generalised gamma convolution, and this paper develops the consequences. The Gibbs state in those experiments is the Riemann zeta distribution: its characteristic function is \u03b6(\u03b2 + it)\/\u03b6(\u03b2), the Euler product is a Levy\u2013Khintchine exponent, unique factorisation is the decomposition of that law into independent prime power jumps, and no transition can occur for Re(s) &amp;gt; 1 because infinitely divisible characteristic functions do not vanish. The main result is that the Riemann Hypothesis holds if and only if \u03a6(\u03bb) = \u03be(1\/2)\/\u03be(1\/2 + \u221a \u03bb) is the Laplace transform of a generalised gamma convolution, necessarily that of X = P k Ek\/\u03b32 k with Ek independent standard exponentials. The Thorin measure is the zero counting measure pushed forward by squaring, with density (4\u03c0 \u221a v) \u22121 log( \u221a v\/2\u03c0); the Levy density is x \u22121\u0398(x) with \u0398 the heat trace of the conjectural Hilbert\u2013Polya operator; the cumulants are (m \u2212 1)!P k \u03b3 \u22122m k ; and \u2212 log \u03a6(\u03bb) \u223c \u03c0N( \u221a \u03bb), whence \u2212 log P(X \u2264 \u03b5) \u223c log2 (1\/\u03b5)\/16\u03b5. Second quantisation unifies the picture: 1\/\u03a6 is the free fermion partition function of a gas with single particle weights \u03b3 \u22122 k and \u03a6 the free boson one, their Taylor coefficients being the elementary and complete homogeneous symmetric functions of those weights, so the hypothesis says exactly that the weights are positive. The fermionic side is a Polya frequency condition, which places the Turan inequalities and the Jensen polynomial results in this frame; the bosonic side is a Stieltjes moment condition computable from \u03be at the central point without any zeros, and verified here through order six. On the apparatus side the probe qubit measures a characteristic function, its dephasing bath spectral density is the Levy measure, and the probe\u2019s time reversal symmetry is the involution whose quotient defines the Thorin variable. These are equivalences rather than a proof strategy, and the circularity that would have to be broken is stated explicitly.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7234498","type":"posted-content","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Thorin Measures and Zeta Zeros"],"author":[{"given":"Nicholas G.","family":"Polson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7234498"}],"items-per-page":15,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":"zeta polynomial of a poset real zeros"}}}